Real Problem

Yesterday code worked.
Today production broke.

Repo has 500 commits 🤯
You don’t know which one caused it.

git bisect will find it in ~9–10 steps instead of 500.


Step 1 — Start bisect
git bisect start
Step 2 — Mark current commit as bad
git bisect bad

(You are currently seeing the bug)

Step 3 — Mark an old working commit as good
git bisect good <commit-id>

Now Git jumps to the middle commit automatically.

Step 4 — Test the code

Run app/tests:

If bug exists:

git bisect bad

If bug not exists:

git bisect good

Repeat…

Final Result

Git prints:

abc1234 is the first bad commit

🎯 You found the exact commit that broke production.

Finish
git bisect reset

Returns to original branch.

DevOps Real Example

Deployment failed after last release.

You can quickly find:

Which merge request broke build

Which config change caused outage

Which commit introduced memory leak

Interview One-Line Answer

git bisect is used to identify the commit that introduced a bug by performing a binary search between a known good and bad commit.

