🔹 Incident Management Levels
L1 – First Line (Frontline / Helpdesk)
Scope: Basic triage and user‑facing support.

Responsibilities:

Acknowledge alerts or tickets.

Perform initial troubleshooting (restart service, check logs).

Escalate if issue is complex.

Example: User reports login issue → L1 checks if service is up, verifies credentials, escalates if deeper.

L2 – Intermediate Support
Scope: Handles more technical issues beyond L1.

Responsibilities:

Deeper investigation into application/service behavior.

Use monitoring tools (Datadog, CloudWatch, Prometheus).

Apply known fixes or patches.

Escalate to L3 if root cause is complex.

Example: Service latency → L2 checks metrics, identifies DB queries slowing down, applies temporary fix.

L3 – Expert / Engineering
Scope: Deep application and infrastructure troubleshooting.

Responsibilities:

Debug code, infrastructure, or configuration issues.

Perform rollbacks, hotfixes, or infra changes.

Work closely with developers and SREs.

Example: Memory leak in microservice → L3 engineers patch code, redeploy service, stabilize infra.

L4 – Architecture / Systemic Fixes
Scope: Long‑term, systemic improvements.

Responsibilities:

Root Cause Analysis (RCA) and architectural redesign.

Implement systemic fixes (e.g., redesign failover, improve CI/CD, add observability).

Prevent recurrence of incidents.

